  "query": "Conduct a comprehensive competitor identification and market share analysis for:\n\n**Industry:** Medical Devices / MedTech / Continuous Glucose Monitoring, Neurostimulation, Brain-Computer Interface\n**Solution Category:** Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices\n**Geography:** North America, Europe (initial); Global (expansion)\n**Business Model:** B2B Component Supply / CDMO (Contract Development and Manufacturing Organization)\n**Target Buyer:** Enterprise OEM Medical Device Manufacturers\n**Problem Context:** LG Innotek (LGIT) has advanced CoF and flexible PCB substrate manufacturing capabilities with 10\u00b5m-level resolution, currently deployed in IT and automotive markets. The company seeks to leverage these capabilities to enter high-value medical device markets\u2014specifically CGM systems, neurostimulation devices, and BCI systems\u2014where thin, flexible, high-density electrode substrates are critical enabling components. The core challenge is determining where in the medical device supply chain LGIT can defensibly participate given its substrate expertise but absence of medical certifications, biosignal IP, and finished-device experience.\n**Market Terms:** medical device flexible circuit substrate, CoF-based medical electrode module, continuous glucose monitoring sensor substrate, neurostimulation electrode array, brain-computer interface electrode substrate, implantable medical device PCB, wearable biosensor flexible electronics, medical grade FPCB component supply, CDMO medical device electronics, high-density electrode substrate medtech, thin-film electrode implantable device, medical electronics contract manufacturing, flexible hybrid electronics medical, biocompatible flexible substrate, micro-electrode array medical device\n**Known Competitors:** CorTec, NeuroNexus, Microprobes for Life Science, NeuroXess, Celestica, Flex Ltd. (Medical CDMO), Dyconex (Precision Flexible Circuits for Implantables), Cirtran / Benchmark Electronics MedTech Division, BrainCo, Nortech Systems\n**Known Segments:** Abbott Diabetes Care (FreeStyle Libre CGM OEM), Abbott Neuromodulation division, Abbott Cardiac Rhythm Management division, Dexcom (CGM OEM), Medtronic Neuromodulation, Boston Scientific neuromodulation, BCI system developers (clinical-stage), Neurostimulation device OEMs\n\n**Known competitors to start with (search for more):** CorTec, NeuroNexus, Microprobes for Life Science, Celestica, NeuroXess, Flex Ltd. (Medical CDMO), Dyconex (Precision Flexible Circuits for Implantables), Cirtran / Benchmark Electronics MedTech Division, BrainCo, Nortech Systems, Sanmina, Heraeus Medical Components, TE Connectivity, Minnetronix Medical (CDMO), BrainGate, Echo (early-stage startup under NDA exploration), Abbott (FreeStyle Libre CGM), Abbott (FreeStyle Libre / Medical Components), Medtronic, Micro Systems Technologies (MST), Blackrock Neurotech, Flex, Nalu Medical, Neuralink, Plexon, Echo (BCI startup, NDA exploration), Ripple Neuro, Integer Holdings, Jabil, Cirtec Medical, Resonant Link / Laborie (implantable module suppliers), Dexcom, Synchron\n\nSearch for: \"top companies in Medical Devices / MedTech\", \"market leaders Medical Devices / MedTech\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices alternatives\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices competitors comparison\", \"Advanced Medical Device Substrates, Flexible Circuit Components, and Electrode Modules for Wearable and Implantable Medical Devices pricing\", \"Medical Devices / MedTech market share\".\n\nResearch and report on ALL of the following. Cite specific sources for every data point.\n\n**A. Competitor Identification**\n1. **Direct competitor list**: Named direct competitors currently serving this market. For each, include: what they do, who they target, positioning, pricing model (if public), funding stage, estimated revenue or scale indicators.\n2. **Substitute and workaround list**: Named substitute products, adjacent vendors, manual workflows, or outsourcing options buyers use instead. These are the real competitive alternatives.\n3. **Competitor revenue or market-share estimates**: Public share figures or revenue estimates for relevant competitors. Note the basis for each estimate (filings, analyst reports, traffic analysis, etc.).\n4. **Public inputs supporting share estimates**: Visible assumptions behind share estimates \u2014 customer counts, pricing, traffic, segment exposure.\n5. **Distribution route mix and channel power**: The relative importance of direct, partner, marketplace, OEM, or distributor-led routes, and who holds leverage in each.\n\nFor each competitor, use a consistent format with the company name in bold. Be specific \u2014 name companies, cite sources, provide concrete details. If pricing is not public, say so.",
